# Type Annotations & Type Inference { .heading-no-number }

> For a progressive introduction, see [The Koka Book: Side Effects](../tutorial/effects.html).
> This guide focuses on type annotations, references, and state.

This guide covers how Koka's type system works: how to write type annotations,
how the type inference engine fills them in when omitted, and how _effect types_
track side-effects in function signatures.

> [!NOTE]
> This page is about the _type system_ — annotations, inference, and effects.
> For how to _define_ your own types (structs, sum types), see
> [Algebraic Data Types](adts.html).

---

```koka {.hidden}
import std/num/random 
```

## Basic Type Annotations

Function parameter types and return types are written with `: type`:

```koka
fun square( x : int ) : int
  x * x
```

Local variable types are usually inferred:

```koka
fun encode( s : string, shift : int )
  s.list.map fn(c)
    if c < 'a' || c > 'z' then return c
    val base = (c - 'a').int   // inferred: int
    val rot  = (base + shift) % 26  // inferred: int
    (rot.char + 'a')
```

Hover over identifiers in an IDE or the online playground to see inferred types.

---

## Effect Types

Every function in Koka carries an _effect_ in its type, describing what side-effects it may perform. The general shape is:

```unchecked
(args) -> <effects> result
```

The absence of effects is `:total` (pure functions with no side-effects):

```koka
fun square1( x : int ) : total int   { x*x }
```

The `:total` effect is usually omitted — writing `: int` as the return type implies `:total`.

Other built-in effects:

| Effect | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `total` | Pure, always terminates |
| `exn` | May raise an exception |
| `div` | May not terminate (diverge) |
| `console` | May write to the console |
| `ndet` | Non-deterministic |
| `io` | Unrestricted I/O |
| `st<h>` | Stateful (heap `h`) |

```koka
fun square2( x : int ) : console int { println( "side-effect!" ); x*x }
fun square3( x : int ) : div int     { x * square3( x ) }
fun square4( x : int ) : exn int     { throw( "oops" ); x*x }
```

---

## Wildcard Effects

If you don't want to spell out the full effect, use `_` or `_e` as a wildcard:

```koka
fun square6( x : int ) : _e int
  println("I didn't write down the effect")
  x*x
```

Hover over `square6` to see what `_e` was inferred as.

---

## Combining Effects

Functions can have multiple effects written as a row `<e1, e2, ...>`:

```unchecked
import std/num/random 
```
```koka
fun combine-effects()
  val i = srandom-int() // ndet
  throw("oops")         // exn
  combine-effects()     // div
```

Koka infers the combined effect `<div,exn,ndet>` (or `:io` in some contexts). The built-in alias `:pure = <div,exn>` represents Haskell-style "purity with exceptions".

---

## Polymorphic Effects

Functions can be _polymorphic_ over effects. For example, `map` works for both pure and effectful functions:

```unchecked
map : (xs : list<a>, f : (a) -> e b) -> e list<b>
```

The effect `e` of the result matches the effect of the function `f` passed in. If `f` is `total`, `map` is `total`. If `f` uses `:console`, so does `map`.

The `while` loop is a plain function, polymorphic over effects:

```unchecked
while : ( pred : () -> <div|e> bool, action : () -> <div|e> () ) -> <div|e> ()
```

The `<div|e>` notation means `div` _plus_ an open row `e` of zero or more additional effects — not just one specific effect. The full effect is the union of `div` and whatever `e` turns out to be.

When this appears in a _parameter_ type (like the `pred` and `action` arguments above), it means the passed function may be called in a context that has _at most_ those effects — but the actual function might need fewer. Koka infers the tightest effect row that satisfies all constraints at the call site, so you rarely need to think about this explicitly.

---

## Value Types vs Reference Types

By default, all data types in Koka are immutable and heap-allocated. Small non-recursive types are automatically treated as _value types_ and passed on the stack:

```koka
value struct argb{ alpha: int; color-red: int; color-green: int; color-blue: int }
```

The `value` keyword forces stack allocation. This avoids heap allocation and reference counting overhead for small data.

---

## Local Mutable State and Effect Erasure

Koka's `var` keyword declares a _local mutable variable_ scoped to the current function.
When the state cannot escape the function, Koka automatically discharges the stateful effect,
making the function appear pure from the outside:

```koka
fun fib2(n)
  var x := 0
  var y := 1
  repeat(n)
    val y0 = y
    y := x + y
    x := y0
  x
```

Even though `fib2` mutates `x` and `y` internally, its inferred type is `total int` — the
state is fully local and cannot escape. Koka automatically applies `run` (similar to
Haskell's `runST`) to discharge the `:st<h>` effect.

> [!NOTE]
> This automatic effect erasure applies only to `var`-based local state. First-class
> heap references created with `ref(...)` retain their `:st<h>` effect, since a reference
> value could escape and be observed by callers. However, if you know your `ref` values
> don't escape, you can manually call `run` to discharge the `:st<h>` effect yourself.

Local `var` variables also cannot be passed to other functions (they are always dereferenced
on use), and their lifetime cannot exceed their lexical scope — properties that make the
automatic `run` transformation safe.

By contrast, first-class heap references really are values that can escape. A direct translation of
the classic mutable Fibonacci loop looks like this:

```koka
fun fib3(n)
  val x = ref(0)
  val y = ref(1)
  repeat(n)
    val y0 = !y
    y := !x + !y
    x := y0
  !x
```

This style is sometimes necessary because references are first-class, but local `var` bindings are usually
preferred when possible: they are syntactically lighter, and their scoped semantics interact better with
effect handlers and resumptions.

At the type level, references live in a heap `h`, and stateful effects are grouped under `st<h>`.
More specifically, allocation contributes `heap<h>`, reading contributes `read<h>`, and mutation contributes
`write<h>`. In most user-facing signatures these are discussed together via the alias `st<h>`.

A concrete larger example is Garsia-Wachs tree construction in
`samples/basic/garsia-wachs.kk`: it uses internal mutable references across helper functions,
while still presenting a pure interface at the top level.

